# Chapter Three: Beneath the Surface

The heavy oak doors of the Kashyap mansion closed softly against the biting Manali cold. Without waking Ira, Shiv scooped her up into his arms, carrying her effortlessly through the grand, dimly lit corridors upstairs until he laid her down gently in the guest room. Leaving strict instructions for a domestic staff member to tend to her, Shiv turned on his heel and headed straight for the study room.

A family meeting was already assembled. Sitting around the dark mahogany desk were his adoptive parents: Pratap Kashyap, the formidable owner of a vast hotel chain and businesses spanning across India, and his wife Aasha Kashyap, a renowned socialite deeply devoted to social work who managed the family empire alongside her husband. Beside them stood Sindhura and Mandhira, their first-year college twin sisters, who looked up with sharp, curious eyes. KK stood quietly by the door, his posture rigid and alert.

In a clipped, emotionless voice, Shiv laid out the bare facts of what had transpired in the pine forest near the Kali temple—omitting the blood-soaked violence for the family's peace of mind, but making it clear that a stranger had needed his protection from Bagga's men.

Once the briefing was finished and the family dispersed with lingering looks of concern, Shiv walked back down the quiet hallway to check on the guest room.

The room was dark. As Shiv stepped closer to the bed, he heard a muffled, terrified whimper. Ira was thrashing against the sheets, caught in the grip of a violent nightmare.

"Papa, no, please... it hurts," she sobbed, her voice cracking with raw terror. "Papa, please, I beg of you..."

The words hit Shiv straight in his core. They echoed the phantom ghosts of his own childhood, making it painfully clear that Ira carried a deep, agonizing wound from her past—one that tonight's violence had brutally ripped open.

Abruptly, Ira gasped and bolted upright, drenched in cold sweat, her chest heaving. In the shadows, her panicked eyes landed on Shiv. She froze, staring into those cold gray eyes. Yet, instead of terrifying her, his steady, unyielding gaze made her feel unnaturally safe and warm.

Without a word, Shiv stepped closer. He sat down gently on the edge of the mattress, pulling out a first-aid kit. With slow, remarkably gentle hands for a man so cold, he cleaned and bandaged the scrapes on her right hand and temple. His quiet, grounded presence slowly anchored her swirling panic.

Later that night, before parting ways, they exchanged quiet, lingering goodbyes. As Shiv walked back down the empty hallway toward his own room, he felt a strange, unfamiliar shifting deep inside his chest—something had permanently moved. And in the guest room, for the very first time in her entire lifetime, Ira fell asleep feeling truly safe with a man.
